#opener("The PiForma’s familiar case is the starting point, not the specification of the computer inside it. This chapter introduces the parts and their jobs. Appendix A keeps the finer details close at hand without requiring you to read a wiring inventory before switching on.")
#heading(level:2,"The monitor that became a computer")
#para([#text("The case began as an Apple IIc Monitor. Its original CRT had been removed before this build. The plastic case, metal chassis, power switch, AC inlet, and rear openings were reused; the computer and display inside are different equipment.")])
#para([#text("This is a conversion, not a restoration of a stock Macintosh. The old case was a good place to start and a rather particular place to fit everything else.")])
#wide-illustration("2-1", "inside-1.png", "The internal assembly, with the Raspberry Pi, display hardware, power adapter, 5V power rail and ordinary internal speaker identified.", width:366.000pt)
#heading(level:2,"Raspberry Pi, memory and storage")
#para([#text("A Raspberry Pi runs the programs. RAM holds the work the computer is doing now; the microSD storage card keeps the operating system, applications, and saved files when power is off. Saving transfers your work into a file that can remain after shutdown.")])
#para([#text("The computer runs Raspberry Pi OS beneath its Macintosh-inspired appearance. Appendix A lists the hardware and software specifications.")])
#heading(level:2,"The display and source selector")
#para([#text("An LCD screen replaces the original monitor display. The Raspberry Pi supplies its picture over HDMI. A rear RCA input supplies composite video from an external source: a VCR, an older games console or anything else with a composite video output. Its sound comes in separately, through the external audio adapter.")])
#para([#text("The screen in this first revision is, frankly, of modest quality: a little dim, with colors that are not quite right, and neither brightness nor color can be adjusted from the computer. We apologize for the economy. You make do with what you have.")])
#para([#text("Each press of the rear source selector moves the screen to its next input. Only two are in use: HDMI, for the Raspberry Pi, and AV1, for the composite RCA input. The selector also passes AV2 and VGA, which have nothing connected. A small label at the top left of the screen names the current input. Press until it shows HDMI to return to the Pi’s picture.")])
#pagebreak(weak:true)
#heading(level:2,"The battery and charging paths")
#para([#text("The internal battery lets you use the PiForma without a power cord. The intended external power cable supplies its charger, and the battery can charge while the computer's physical switch is off. The switch does not disconnect the charger from external power. Appendix A describes the internal power path.")])
#para([#text("An external plunger reaches the battery’s own button. Press it to check the charge, and sometimes to wake the pack. The normal check is to press the rear plunger and read the percentage through the top slits. The top latch can also be unlocked and lifted for a closer look; Chapter 1 describes the optional opening procedure.")])
#wide-illustration("2-2", "inside-3.png", "The open top reveals the wireless charging receiver, battery-button plunger and battery display. The battery is at 84% in this photograph. Its display can also indicate charging from the wireless receiver, but is hidden while the computer is packed in the bag. For the normal check, read through the top slits as described in Chapter 1.", width:366.000pt)
#heading(level:2,"System sound and startup chimes")
#para([#text("The startup chime and ordinary system audio are separate. Ordinary sound plays through the internal speaker behind the screen or, when selected, through the rear audio output socket. A separate sound module and speaker play the startup chime; the rear selector chooses among its sounds. Some positions are silent or empty.")])
